[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. The reports merged into it
describe three different bugs. The fix queued for the parser closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket now. Do not close them with the parser fix.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Counts come from `reports-4471.json` after removing ids the export lists twice. The cause of a
report comes from its tenant and the way it came in (`causes.ts`), not from the intake tag, which
is a guess from the reporter's browser.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Every tenant in #4471 writes the day
first. A date like 7 April comes out as 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names, and the fix queued for it.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** Tenants on booking-v3 write the day
first, so the form reads the date day first. Their people type what the hint says, month first.
The date comes out swapped through the form even after the parser is fixed. Only reports through
the `form` channel at booking-v3 tenants.

**`one-day`: dates shift by one day, not by a swap.** These are the reports the desk already noted
as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). Their bodies describe a meeting one day early or late, a
different day in Lisbon and in Toronto, an invite that arrived before the booking, one month (June)
off by one day. That is a time zone or daylight-saving bug, not a date order bug. The parser fix does
not touch it.

## Why they were merged

Decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone. All three
problems arrive with the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so all three
landed in one ticket. The subject says what the reporter saw. The body, the tenant and the channel
say what broke.

## What to do

- Track each problem on its own ticket (table above).
- Before closing a ticket, check the reports still arriving against its cause, not its subject.
- Decision 0001 needs a successor record: split on cause (`sameCause`), not on subject.

